---
import SocialShare from '~/components/common/SocialShare.astro';
import V9Layout from '~/layouts/V9Layout.astro';
import Content from '~/components/v9/content/Content.astro';
import { publicLinks } from '~/data/v9/preview-links';
import { fetchPosts } from '~/utils/blog';
import { getPermalink } from '~/utils/permalinks';
const post = (await fetchPosts()).find(
  (post) => post.slug === 'blog/designing-ai-for-high-stakes-technical-assessment'
)!;

const shareUrl = new URL(Astro.url.pathname, 'https://www.tribera.ai').href;
---

<V9Layout
  title="Designing AI for high-stakes assessment — tribera"
  description="Where we drew the line between what the engine decides and what a person decides — and why the line sits where it does."
  headerLinkOverrides={publicLinks}
  footerLinkOverrides={publicLinks}
  openGraphType="article"
  image="~/assets/images/blog/ai-technical-assessment.png"
>
  <Content kind="article">
    <div class="reading" aria-hidden="true"><span class="reading__f"></span></div>

    <header class="ahead band">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="ahead__meta">
          <time datetime={post.publishDate.toISOString()}
            >{
              post.publishDate.toLocaleDateString('en-GB', {
                day: 'numeric',
                month: 'short',
                year: 'numeric',
                timeZone: 'UTC',
              })
            }</time
          ><i>·</i>
          {post.category && <a href={getPermalink(post.category.slug, 'category')}>{post.category.title}</a>}<i>·</i
          ><span>{post.readingTime} read</span>
        </p>
        <h1>Designing AI for high-stakes assessment</h1>
        <p class="ahead__sub">A model can read a transcript. It cannot tell you whether the person meant it.</p>
        <div class="ahead__by">
          <span class="ahead__ph" id="byPh" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="ahead__who"><b>Abhishek Prasad</b>Co-founder &amp; CBO · built the signal engine</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="band band--w">
      <div class="wrap">
        <article class="art">
          <p class="lede">
            The useful question was never whether a model could score an interview. It was which parts of that judgment
            we were willing to let it make.
          </p>

          <p>
            A language model reads a transcript better than most people expect. It will find the moment an answer went
            from specific to general. It will notice that a candidate described a system in the first person plural for
            four minutes and then switched to "I" when the question got easier. It is genuinely good at this, and it is
            fast.
          </p>

          <p>What it cannot do is tell you whether the person meant it.</p>

          <h2>Three things we let the engine do</h2>

          <p>
            <b>Read the profile.</b> A hundred-plus signals before anyone picks up a phone — what a CV describes versus what
            it implies, tenure patterns, the difference between a title and a scope. This is pattern work at volume and a
            machine is simply better at it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Write the questions.</b> Questions are shaped by the role and the candidate’s experience, which means the engine
            has to understand the claim well enough to turn it into something only the person who did it could answer. "Built
            microservices handling two million requests a day" becomes "which service did you split off first, and what broke
            when you did".
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Score the transcript.</b> Against a rubric that was set before the interview, with the evidence attached to
            each dimension. Consistency is the whole argument here: the same answer gets the same score on a Friday afternoon
            as it does on a Tuesday morning, which is not true of people.
          </p>

          <blockquote class="pull">
            The engine works both ends.
            <em>The expert does the part in between.</em>
          </blockquote>

          <h2>One thing we will not let it do</h2>

          <p>
            It cannot reject anyone. Not as a policy we might revisit — as a property of how the thing is built. The
            engine surfaces and explains; a person reads the transcript and writes the verdict; you make the call. There
            is no path through the system where a candidate is removed without a human having read why.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is not primarily an ethics position, though it is defensible on those grounds. It is that the failure
            mode we most wanted to avoid is the one nobody notices: a model quietly filtering out a category of person
            for a reason nobody articulated, month after month, with the numbers looking fine throughout.
          </p>

          <h2>What we got wrong first</h2>

          <p>
            Our earliest version scored candidates against a general bar — a good backend engineer, in the abstract. It
            worked, in the sense that the rankings were sensible and defensible.
          </p>

          <p>
            It was also close to useless, because there is no such thing as a good backend engineer in the abstract. The
            scores were stable and the hires were not, and it took us longer than it should have to see that the
            stability was the problem. A bar that does not move between a payments platform and a consumer app is not a
            bar; it is an average.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the rubric moved to the client. Six to eleven dimensions, weighted at intake, and the weighting is
            visible to everyone including the candidate. The model got less clever and the results got better, which is
            a lesson that generalises further than we expected.
          </p>

          <h2>Explainability is not a feature</h2>

          <p>
            Every score carries the rubric, the evidence and the transcript line it came from. Partly because a hiring
            manager who cannot see the reasoning will not trust the number, and partly because a candidate who is ranked
            third deserves to know on what basis.
          </p>

          <p>
            The test we hold to is simple and slightly uncomfortable: <b
              >could we defend this score to the person it belongs to, in a room, without a slide?</b
            > If not, it does not ship.
          </p>

          <p class="signoff">Belong. Build. Become.</p>
        </article>
        <div class="aft">
          <div class="atags">
            {
              post.tags?.map((tag) => (
                <a class="atag" href={getPermalink(tag.slug, 'tag')}>
                  {tag.title}
                </a>
              ))
            }
          </div>
          <SocialShare class="ashare" url={shareUrl} text={post.title} />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap band" id="related">
      <div class="shead" style="grid-template-columns:1fr;text-align:center;justify-items:center;gap:8px">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--red" data-v9-reveal>Keep reading</p>
          <h2 class="h2" data-v9-reveal style="--d:60ms;max-width:none;margin-bottom:clamp(16px,2vw,24px)">
            Three more that came out of real mandates.
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p class="dek" data-v9-reveal style="--d:100ms;max-width:none">
          Or go back to <a
            href="/blog"
            style="color:var(--red);
    text-decoration:none;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(218,0,7,.35)"
            >everything we’ve published</a
          >.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rel" data-v9-reveal style="--d:140ms">
        <a class="relc" href="/blog/rethinking-hiring-metrics-signal-efficiency"
          ><p class="relc__k">Hiring strategy</p><p class="relc__t">Rethinking hiring metrics</p><p class="relc__x">
            Time-to-hire measures your speed. Interview-to-hire measures your aim.
          </p></a
        ><a class="relc" href="/blog/designing-for-trust-privacy-transparency-respect"
          ><p class="relc__k">Candidate experience</p><p class="relc__t">Designing for trust</p><p class="relc__x">
            Most people looking for a job already have one.
          </p></a
        ><a class="relc" href="/blog/signal-vs-noise-technical-hiring"
          ><p class="relc__k">Technical hiring</p><p class="relc__t">Signal versus noise</p><p class="relc__x">
            Most teams spend their week evaluating people who should never have entered the process.
          </p></a
        >
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="trust band" id="cta">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="sub" style="text-align:center;display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center">
          <p class="sub__k">Newsletter in preparation</p>
          <p class="sub__h">Get notified when it is ready.</p>
          <p class="sub__s">Leave your email and we will send one note when the field notes newsletter opens.</p>
          <form
            class="apply__form"
            data-newsletter-form
            action="/.netlify/functions/site-inquiry"
            method="post"
            enctype="multipart/form-data"
            style="display:flex;justify-content:center;width:fit-content;margin-inline:auto"
          >
            <input type="hidden" name="form_type" value="newsletter" /><input
              type="hidden"
              name="form_loaded_at"
            /><input
              type="text"
              name="botcheck"
              tabindex="-1"
              autocomplete="off"
              aria-hidden="true"
              style="position:absolute;left:-9999px"
            />
            <div class="apply__row apply__row--2">
              <input
                class="apply__mail"
                type="email"
                name="email"
                required
                placeholder="your@email.com"
                aria-label="Your email address"
              />
              <button class="apply__go" type="submit" disabled>Notify me<span class="apply__ar">→</span></button>
            </div>
          </form>
          <noscript
            ><p class="apply__fine">
              For updates, email <a href="mailto:hello@tribera.ai?subject=Field%20notes%20notifications"
                >hello@tribera.ai</a
              >.
            </p></noscript
          ><p class="apply__fine" data-newsletter-status aria-live="polite">We never share the list.</p><script
            src="/scripts/newsletter-signup.js"
            defer></script>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band band--w close" id="contact">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="shead" style="grid-template-columns:1fr;text-align:center;justify-items:center;gap:8px">
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--red" data-v9-reveal>Two doors</p>
            <h2 class="h2" data-v9-reveal style="max-width:none">Two doors. Same room.</h2>
          </div>
          <p class="dek" data-v9-reveal style="--d:60ms;max-width:none">
            If you have a role you’re stuck on, or you’re the one being interviewed, <b
              >both of those start the same way.</b
            >
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="doors" data-v9-reveal style="--d:110ms">
          <div class="door">
            <p class="door__k">If you’re hiring</p>
            <p class="door__t">Three names you’d take any of.</p>
            <p class="door__d">
              A hundred signals, a senior expert on each shortlisted person, and the reasoning behind every rank.
            </p>
            <p class="door__next"><span>Next</span> A 45-minute intake call. Three names back inside 36 hours.</p>
            <a href="/" class="btn btn--dark">See how one role ran</a>
          </div>
          <div class="door">
            <p class="door__k">If you’re looking</p>
            <p class="door__t">One conversation, then teams come to you.</p>
            <p class="door__d">
              Half an hour with someone who has done your job. Anonymous until you decide otherwise.
            </p>
            <p class="door__next"><span>Next</span> Fifteen minutes to set up, and you keep the score.</p>
            <a href="/candidates" class="btn btn--ghost">Create your profile</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="bbb2" data-v9-reveal><span>Belong</span><i></i><span>Build</span><i></i><span>Become</span></p>
        <p class="bbb2__s" data-v9-reveal>Tribe + Era. A hire only works if both sides were right about each other.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <script>
      /* ══ INDUSTRIES: the same six dimensions, any two sectors ══ */
      (function () {
        const W = {
          'consumer-digital-commerce': { n: 'Consumer & digital commerce', w: [30, 24, 8, 22, 6, 10] },
          'banking-financial-services': { n: 'Banking & financial services', w: [20, 14, 30, 10, 18, 8] },
          'enterprise-technology': { n: 'Enterprise technology', w: [26, 26, 10, 20, 10, 8] },
          'product-saas': { n: 'Product & SaaS', w: [22, 18, 6, 20, 8, 26] },
          'ai-data': { n: 'AI & data', w: [30, 26, 10, 16, 10, 8] },
          cybersecurity: { n: 'Cybersecurity', w: [24, 16, 32, 12, 10, 6] },
          'healthcare-life-sciences': { n: 'Healthcare & life sciences', w: [18, 12, 30, 10, 22, 8] },
          pharmaceutical: { n: 'Pharmaceutical', w: [16, 10, 34, 8, 24, 8] },
          manufacturing: { n: 'Manufacturing', w: [22, 14, 22, 18, 14, 10] },
          'industrial-engineering': { n: 'Industrial & engineering', w: [24, 18, 18, 18, 14, 8] },
          'global-capability-centres': { n: 'Global capability centres', w: [22, 18, 12, 20, 10, 18] },
          'private-equity-portfolio': { n: 'Private equity portfolio', w: [20, 14, 10, 32, 8, 16] },
        };
        const D = [
          'Core depth',
          'Systems design',
          'Controls and risk',
          'Delivery speed',
          'Documentation',
          'Stakeholder handling',
        ];
        const a = document.querySelector<HTMLSelectElement>('#secA'),
          b = document.querySelector<HTMLSelectElement>('#secB'),
          ft = document.getElementById('cmpFt');
        if (!a || !b) return;
        function paint() {
          if (!a || !b || !ft) return;
          const A = W[a.value],
            B = W[b.value];
          let mx = 0,
            gap = -1,
            gi = 0;
          D.forEach(function (_, i) {
            mx = Math.max(mx, A.w[i], B.w[i]);
            const g = Math.abs(A.w[i] - B.w[i]);
            if (g > gap) {
              gap = g;
              gi = i;
            }
          });
          D.forEach(function (n, i) {
            {
              const node = document.getElementById('fa' + i);
              if (node) node.style.width = (A.w[i] / mx) * 100 + '%';
            }
            {
              const node = document.getElementById('fb' + i);
              if (node) node.style.width = (B.w[i] / mx) * 100 + '%';
            }
            {
              const node = document.getElementById('va' + i);
              if (node) node.textContent = A.w[i];
            }
            {
              const node = document.getElementById('vb' + i);
              if (node) node.textContent = B.w[i];
            }
            {
              const node = document.getElementById('bw' + i);
              if (node) node.classList.toggle('key', i === gi);
            }
          });
          if (A.n === B.n) {
            ft.innerHTML = 'Same sector both sides. <b>Pick two and the bar moves.</b>';
            return;
          }
          ft.innerHTML =
            '<b>' +
            D[gi] +
            '</b> moves from ' +
            Math.min(A.w[gi], B.w[gi]) +
            '% to ' +
            Math.max(A.w[gi], B.w[gi]) +
            '% — a ' +
            gap +
            '-point swing, and <b>a different person wins</b>.';
        }
        a.addEventListener('change', paint);
        b.addEventListener('change', paint);
        paint();
      })();
    </script>
  </Content>
</V9Layout>
